           \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools/generate_authors_table.py\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools/circle\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/conftest.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/conf.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/tutorial\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/sphinxext\n"}, {"content": "Based on the issue description, we need to focus on the `fetch_openml` functionality which is likely located in the `sklearn.datasets` directory. Let me search for this function:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['fetch_openml']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/datasets/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern 'sklearn/datasets/**/*.py'. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"fetch_openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `fetch_openml`.\n```\n664 | def fetch_openml(\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also search for the data download related functions:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_download_data_to_bunch', '_get_data_description_by_id']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/datasets/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern 'sklearn/datasets/**/*.py'. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"_download_data_to_bunch\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_get_data_description_by_id\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_get_data_description_by_id`.\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check for any data loading related functionality:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/datasets/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern 'sklearn/datasets/**/*.py'. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check for the actual data handling:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_convert_arff_data']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/datasets/**/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern 'sklearn/datasets/**/*.py'. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"_convert_arff_data\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_convert_arff_data`.\n```\n233 | def _convert_arff_data(\n234 |     arff: ArffContainerType,\n235 |     col_slice_x: List[int],\n236 |     col_slice_y: List[int],\n237 |     shape: Optional[Tuple] = None\n238 | ) -> Tuple:\n239 |     \"\"\"\n240 |     converts the arff object into the appropriate matrix type (np.array or\n241 |     scipy.sparse.csr_matrix) based on the 'data part' (i.e., in the\n242 |     liac-arff dict, the object from the 'data' key)\n243 | \n244 |     Parameters\n245 |     ----------\n246 |     arff : dict\n247 |         As obtained from liac-arff object.\n248 | \n249 |     col_slice_x : list\n250 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n251 |         as X data\n252 | \n253 |     col_slice_y : list\n254 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n255 |         as y data\n256 | \n257 |     Returns\n258 |     -------\n259 |     X : np.array or scipy.sparse.csr_matrix\n260 |     y : np.array\n261 |     \"\"\"\n262 |     arff_data = arff['data']\n263 |     if isinstance(arff_data, Generator):\n264 |         if shape is None:\n265 |             raise ValueError(\n266 |                 \"shape must be provided when arr['data'] is a Generator\"\n267 |             )\n268 |         if shape[0] == -1:\n269 |             count = -1\n270 |         else:\n271 |             count = shape[0] * shape[1]\n272 |         data = np.fromiter(itertools.chain.from_iterable(arff_data),\n273 |                            dtype='float64', count=count)\n274 |         data = data.reshape(*shape)\n275 |         X = data[:, col_slice_x]\n276 |         y = data[:, col_slice_y]\n277 |         return X, y\n278 |     elif isinstance(arff_data, tuple):\n279 |         arff_data_X = _split_sparse_columns(arff_data, col_slice_x)\n280 |         num_obs = max(arff_data[1]) + 1\n281 |         X_shape = (num_obs, len(col_slice_x))\n282 |         X = scipy.sparse.coo_matrix(\n283 |             (arff_data_X[0], (arff_data_X[1], arff_data_X[2])),\n284 |             shape=X_shape, dtype=np.float64)\n285 |         X = X.tocsr()\n286 |         y = _sparse_data_to_array(arff_data, col_slice_y)\n287 |         return X, y\n288 |     else:\n289 |         # This should never happen\n290 |         raise ValueError('Unexpected Data Type obtained from arff.')\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check for any data validation or hashing related imports:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['import hashlib', 'from hashlib import']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_sha256\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_pkl_filepath\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_sha256\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_pkl_filepath\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_sha256\nsklearn/datasets/_base.py:_pkl_filepath\n##Searching for term \"import hashlib\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/datasets/_base.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | Base IO code for all datasets\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | \n   5 | # Copyright (c) 2007 David Cournapeau <cournape@gmail.com>\n   6 | #               2010 Fabian Pedregosa <fabian.pedregosa@inria.fr>\n   7 | #               2010 Olivier Grisel <olivier.grisel@ensta.org>\n   8 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n   9 | import os\n  10 | import csv\n  11 | import shutil\n  12 | from collections import namedtuple\n  13 | from os import environ, listdir, makedirs\n  14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, expanduser, isdir, join, splitext\n  15 | import hashlib\n  16 | \n  17 | from ..utils import Bunch\n  18 | from ..utils import check_random_state\n  19 | from ..utils import check_pandas_support\n  20 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n  21 | \n  22 | import numpy as np\n  23 | \n  24 | from urllib.request import urlretrieve\n  25 | \n  26 | RemoteFileMetadata = namedtuple('RemoteFileMetadata',\n  27 |                                 ['filename', 'url', 'checksum'])\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/metrics/