olate/_bsplines.py`, the design_matrix function will need to be checked for compatibility with sparse matrices.\n\n4. The sparse matrix implementation in `scipy/sparse/_compressed.py` provides the base functionality that can be utilized.\n\n5. Basic sparse array initialization in `scipy/sparse/_base.py` would be useful for reference.\n\nThe core changes would involve modifying how the transformation matrix is computed and returned, particularly in the transform method of SplineTransformer, while ensuring it maintains compatibility with the existing estimator architecture.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved the task, rewrite your final answer into the required location-only format, output ONLY the location block, and then call `finish` to finish.\nIMPORTANT: YOU SHOULD NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDaLIC raw evidence tools remain available throughout the investigation.\nIf your current evidence is either too broad, too few or incomplete, call `get_trace_artifacts` or `get_trace_data_dependencies` again with more precise filters before finishing.\n"}, {"content": "Let me verify the completeness of the found locations and analyze additional dependencies.\n\nFirst, let me check for any additional references to sparse matrix implementation and B-splines in the codebase:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sparse', 'bspline', 'design_matrix']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot\nsklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:paired_distances\nsklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:linear_kernel\nsklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:binarize\nsklearn/metrics/cluster/_supervised.py:contingency_matrix\nsklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:QuantileTransformer.transform\nsklearn/impute/_base.py:MissingIndicator.__init__\nsklearn/impute/_base.py:MissingIndicator\nsklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:QuantileTransformer._more_tags\nsklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:QuantileTransformer.inverse_transform\nsklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:cosine_similarity\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.fit\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._more_tags\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._get_base_knot_positions\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.fit\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:PolynomialFeatures.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.fit_transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_pos_label_consistency\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.fit\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.fit_transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD\nsklearn/datasets/_arff_parser.py:_pandas_arff_parser\nsklearn/datasets/_arff_parser.py:_post_process_frame\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.fit\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._more_tags\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:PolynomialFeatures.transform\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer._get_base_knot_positions\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py:SplineTransformer.fit\nsklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot\n##Searching for term \"sparse\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==============================\n 3 | Lasso on dense and sparse data\n 4 | ==============================\n 5 | \n 6 | We show that linear_model.Lasso provides the same results for dense and sparse\n 7 | data and that in the case of sparse data the speed is improved.\n 8 | \n 9 | \"\"\"\n10 | \n11 | from time import time\n12 | from scipy import sparse\n13 | from scipy import linalg\n14 | \n15 | from sklearn.datasets import make_regression\n16 | from sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\n17 | \n18 | \n19 | # %%\n20 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Dense data\n21 | # -----------------------------------------------------\n22 | #\n23 | # We create a linear regression problem that is suitable for the Lasso,\n24 | # that is to say, with more features than samples. We then store the data\n25 | # matrix in both dense (the usual) and sparse format, and train a Lasso on\n26 | # each. We compute the runtime of both and check that they learned the\n27 | # same model by computing the Euclidean norm of the difference between the\n28 | # coefficients they learned. Because the data is dense, we expect better\n29 | # runtime with a dense data format.\n30 | \n31 | X, y = make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=5000, random_state=0)\n32 | # create a copy of X in sparse format\n33 | X_sp = sparse.coo_matrix(X)\n34 | \n35 | alpha = 1\n36 | sparse_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n37 | dense_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n38 | \n39 | t0 = time()\n40 | sparse_lasso.fit(X_sp, y)\n41 | print(f\"Sparse Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n42 | \n43 | t0 = time()\n44 | dense_lasso.fit(X, y)\n45 | print(f\"Dense Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n46 | \n47 | # compare the regression coefficients\n48 | coeff_diff = linalg.norm(sparse_lasso.coef_ - dense_lasso.coef_)\n49 | print(f\"Distance between coefficients : {coeff_diff:.2e}\")\n50 | \n51 | #\n52 | # %%\n53 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Sparse data\n54 | # ------------------------------------------------------\n55 | #\n56 | # We make the previous problem sparse by replacing all small values with 0\n57 | # and run the same comparisons as above. Because the data is now sparse, we\n58 | # expect the implementation that uses the sparse data format to be faster.\n59 | \n60 | # make a copy of the previous data\n61 | Xs = X.copy()\n62 | # make Xs sparse by replacing the values lower than 2.5 with 0s\n63 | Xs[Xs < 2.5] = 0.0\n64 | # create a copy of Xs in sparse format\n```\n\nFound function `asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/datasets.py:_synth_regression_sparse_dataset`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n101 | def _synth_regression_sparse_dataset(\n102 |     n_samples=10000, n_features=10000, density=0.01, dtype=np.float32\n103 | ):\n104 |     X = sp.random(\n105 |         m=n_samples, n=n_features, density=density, format=\"csr\", random_state=0\n106 |     )\n107 |     X.data = np.random.RandomState(0).randn(X.getnnz())\n108 |     X = X.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n109 |     coefs = sp.random(m=n_features, n=1, density=0.5, random_state=0)\n110 |     coefs.data = np.random.RandomState(0).randn(coefs.getnnz())\n111 |     y = X.dot(coefs.toarray()).reshape(-1)\n112 |     y += 0.2 * y.std() * np.random.randn(n_samples)\n113 | \n114 |     X, X_val, y, y_val = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.1, random_state=0)\n115 |     return X, X_val, y, y_val\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/kernel_approximation.py:AdditiveChi2Sampler._transform_sparse`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n829 | def _transform_sparse(X, sample_steps, sample_interval):\n830 |         indices = X.indices.copy()\n831 |         indptr = X.indptr.copy()\n832 | \n833 |         data_step = np.sqrt(X.data * sample_interval)\n834 |         X_step = sp.csr_matrix(\n835 |             (data_step, indices, indptr), shape=X.shape, dtype=X.dtype, copy=False\n836 |         )\n837 |         X_new = [X_step]\n838 | \n839 |         log_step_nz = sample_interval * np.log(X.data)\n840 |         step_nz = 2 * X.data * sample_interval\n841 | \n842 |         for j in range(1, sample_steps):\n843 |             factor_nz = np.sqrt(step_nz / np.cosh(np.pi * j * sample_interval))\n844 | \n845 |             data_step = factor_nz * np.cos(j * log_step_nz)\n846 |             X_step = sp.csr_matrix(\n847 |                 (data_step, indices, indptr), shape=X.shape, dtype=X.dtype, copy=False\n848 |             )\n849 |             X_new.append(X_step)\n850 | \n851 |             data_step = factor_nz * np.sin(j * log_step_nz)\n852 |             X_step = sp.csr_matrix(\n853 |                 (data_step, indices, indptr), shape=X.shape, dtype=X.dtype, copy=False\n854 |             )\n855 |             X_new.append(X_step)\n856 | \n857 |         return sp.hstack(X_new)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/extmath.py:safe_sparse_dot`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n160 | def safe_sparse_dot(a, b, *, dense_output=False):\n161 |     \"\"\"Dot product that handle the sparse matrix case correctly.\n162 | \n163 |     Parameters\n164 |     ----------\n165 |     a : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n166 |     b : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n167 |     dense_output : bool, default=False\n168 |         When False, ``a`` and ``b`` both being sparse will yield sparse output.\n169 |         When True, output will always be a dense array.\n170 | \n171 |     Returns\n172 |     -------\n173 |     dot_product : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n174 |         Sparse if ``a`` and ``b`` are sparse and ``dense_output=False``.\n175 |     \"\"\"\n176 |     if a.ndim > 2 or b.ndim > 2:\n177 |         if sparse.issparse(a):\n178 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies b is 3D+\n179 |             # [i, j] @ [k, ..., l, m, n] -> [i, k, ..., l, n]\n180 |             b_ = np.rollaxis(b, -2)\n181 |             b_2d = b_.reshape((b.shape[-2], -1))\n182 |             ret = a @ b_2d\n183 |             ret = ret.reshape(a.shape[0], *b_.shape[1:])\n184 |         elif sparse.issparse(b):\n185 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies a is 3D+\n186 |             # [k, ..., l, m] @ [i, j] -> [k, ..., l, j]\n187 |             a_2d = a.reshape(-1, a.shape[-1])\n188 |             ret = a_2d @ b\n189 |             ret = ret.reshape(*a.shape[:-1], b.shape[1])\n190 |         else:\n191 |             ret = np.dot(a, b)\n192 |     else:\n193 |         ret = a @ b\n194 | \n195 |     if (\n196 |         sparse.issparse(a)\n197 |         and sparse.issparse(b)\n198 |         and dense_output\n199 |         and hasattr(ret, \"toarray\")\n200 |     ):\n201 |         return ret.toarray()\n202 |     return ret\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:paired_distances`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1165 | def paired_distances(X, Y, *, metric=\"euclidean\", **kwds):\n1166 |     \"\"\"\n1167 |     Compute the paired distances between X and Y.\n1168 | \n1169 |     Compute the distances between (X[0], Y[0]), (X[1], Y[1]), etc...\n1170 | \n1171 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <metrics>`.\n1172 | \n1173 |     Parameters\n1174 |     ----------\n1175 |     X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1176 |         Array 1 for distance computation.\n1177 | \n1178 |     Y : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1179 |         Array 2 for distance computation.\n1180 | \n1181 |     metric : str or callable, default=\"euclidean\"\n1182 |         The metric to use when calculating distance between instances in a\n1183 |         feature array. If metric is a string, it must be one of the options\n1184 |         specified in PAIRED_DISTANCES, including \"euclidean\",\n1185 |         \"manhattan\", or \"cosine\".\n1186 |         Alternatively, if metric is a callable function, it is called on each\n1187 |         pair of instances (rows) and the resulting value recorded. The callable\n1188 |         should take two arrays from `X` as input and return a value indicating\n1189 |         the distance between them.\n1190 | \n1191 |     **kwds : dict\n1192 |         Unused parameters.\n1193 | \n1194 |     Returns\n1195 |     -------\n1196 |     distances : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n1197 |         Returns the distances between the row vectors of `X`\n1198 |         and the row vectors of `Y`.\n1199 | \n1200 |     See Also\n1201 |     --------\n1202 |     pairwise_distances : Computes the distance between every pair of samples.\n1203 | \n1204 |     Examples\n1205 |     --------\n1206 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import paired_distances\n1207 |     >>> X = [[0, 1], [1, 1]]\n1208 |     >>> Y = [[0, 1], [2, 1]]\n1209 |     >>> paired_distances(X, Y)\n1210 |     array([0., 1.])\n1211 |     \"\"\"\n1212 | \n1213 |     if metric in PAIRED_DISTANCES:\n1214 |         func = PAIRED_DISTANCES[metric]\n1215 |         return func(X, Y)\n1216 |     elif callable(metric):\n1217 |         # Check the matrix first (it is usually done by the metric)\n1218 |         X, Y = check_paired_arrays(X, Y)\n1219 |         distances = np.zeros(len(X))\n1220 |         for i in range(len(X)):\n1221 |             distances[i] = metric(X[i], Y[i])\n1222 |         return distances\n1223 |     else:\n1224 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown distance %s\" % metric)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:linear_kernel`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1235 | def linear_kernel(X, Y=None, dense_output=Tr