r.py:make_sparse_spd_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1559 | def make_sparse_spd_matrix(\n1560 |     dim=1,\n1561 |     *,\n1562 |     alpha=0.95,\n1563 |     norm_diag=False,\n1564 |     smallest_coef=0.1,\n1565 |     largest_coef=0.9,\n1566 |     random_state=None,\n1567 | ):\n1568 |     \"\"\"Generate a sparse symmetric definite positive matrix.\n1569 | \n1570 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_generators>`.\n1571 | \n1572 |     Parameters\n1573 |     ----------\n1574 |     dim : int, default=1\n1575 |         The size of the random matrix to generate.\n1576 | \n1577 |     alpha : float, default=0.95\n1578 |         The probability that a coefficient is zero (see notes). Larger values\n1579 |         enforce more sparsity. The value should be in the range 0 and 1.\n1580 | \n1581 |     norm_diag : bool, default=False\n1582 |         Whether to normalize the output matrix to make the leading diagonal\n1583 |         elements all 1.\n1584 | \n1585 |     smallest_coef : float, default=0.1\n1586 |         The value of the smallest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1587 | \n1588 |     largest_coef : float, default=0.9\n1589 |         The value of the largest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1590 | \n1591 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1592 |         Determines random number generation for dataset creation. Pass an int\n1593 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n1594 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1595 | \n1596 |     Returns\n1597 |     -------\n1598 |     prec : sparse matrix of shape (dim, dim)\n1599 |         The generated matrix.\n1600 | \n1601 |     See Also\n1602 |     --------\n1603 |     make_spd_matrix : Generate a random symmetric, positive-definite matrix.\n1604 | \n1605 |     Notes\n1606 |     -----\n1607 |     The sparsity is actually imposed on the cholesky factor of the matrix.\n1608 |     Thus alpha does not translate directly into the filling fraction of\n1609 |     the matrix itself.\n1610 |     \"\"\"\n1611 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n1612 | \n1613 |     chol = -np.eye(dim)\n1614 |     aux = random_state.uniform(size=(dim, dim))\n1615 |     aux[aux < alpha] = 0\n1616 |     aux[aux > alpha] = smallest_coef + (\n1617 |         largest_coef - smallest_coef\n1618 |     ) * random_state.uniform(size=np.sum(aux > alpha))\n1619 |     aux = np.tril(aux, k=-1)\n1620 | \n1621 |     # Permute the lines: we don't want to have asymmetries in the final\n1622 |     # SPD matrix\n1623 |     permutation = random_state.permutation(dim)\n1624 |     aux = aux[permutation].T[permutation]\n1625 |     chol += aux\n1626 |     prec = np.dot(chol.T, chol)\n1627 | \n1628 |     if norm_diag:\n1629 |         # Form the diagonal vector into a row matrix\n1630 |         d = np.diag(prec).reshape(1, prec.shape[0])\n1631 |         d = 1.0 / np.sqrt(d)\n1632 | \n1633 |         prec *= d\n1634 |         prec *= d.T\n1635 | \n1636 |     return prec\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the sparse data handling utilities:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sklearn/utils/extmath.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"sklearn/utils/extmath.py\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/utils/extmath.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/utils/extmath.py`.\n```\n   1 | \"\"\"\n   2 | Extended math utilities.\n   3 | \"\"\"\n   4 | # Authors: Gael Varoquaux\n   5 | #          Alexandre Gramfort\n   6 | #          Alexandre T. Passos\n   7 | #          Olivier Grisel\n   8 | #          Lars Buitinck\n   9 | #          Stefan van der Walt\n  10 | #          Kyle Kastner\n  11 | #          Giorgio Patrini\n  12 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n  13 | \n  14 | import warnings\n  15 | \n  16 | import numpy as np\n  17 | from scipy import linalg, sparse\n  18 | \n  19 | from . import check_random_state\n  20 | from ._logistic_sigmoid import _log_logistic_sigmoid\n  21 | from .sparsefuncs_fast import csr_row_norms\n  22 | from .validation import check_array\n  23 | from ._array_api import get_namespace, _is_numpy_namespace\n  24 | \n  25 | \n  26 | def squared_norm(x):\n  27 |     \"\"\"Squared Euclidean or Frobenius norm of x.\n  28 | \n  29 |     Faster than norm(x) ** 2.\n  30 | \n  31 |     Parameters\n  32 |     ----------\n  33 |     x : array-like\n  34 |         The input array which could be either be a vector or a 2 dimensional array.\n  35 | \n  36 |     Returns\n  37 |     -------\n  38 |     float\n  39 |         The Euclidean norm when x is a vector, the Frobenius norm when x\n  40 |         is a matrix (2-d array).\n  41 |     \"\"\"\n  42 |     x = np.ravel(x, order=\"K\")\n  43 |     if np.issubdtype(x.dtype, np.integer):\n  44 |         warnings.warn(\n  45 |             (\n  46 |                 \"Array type is integer, np.dot may overflow. \"\n  47 |                 \"Data should be float type to avoid this issue\"\n  48 |             ),\n  49 |             UserWarning,\n  50 |         )\n  51 |     return np.dot(x, x)\n  52 | \n  53 | \n  54 | def row_norms(X, squared=False):\n  55 |     \"\"\"Row-wise (squared) Euclidean norm of X.\n  56 | \n  57 |     Equivalent to np.sqrt((X * X).sum(axis=1)), but also supports sparse\n  58 |     matrices and does not create an X.shape-sized temporary.\n  59 | \n  60 |     Performs no input validation.\n  61 | \n  62 |     Parameters\n  63 |     ----------\n  64 |     X : array-like\n  65 |         The input array.\n  66 |     squared : bool, default=False\n  67 |         If True, return squared norms.\n  68 | \n  69 |     Returns\n  70 |     -------\n  71 |     array-like\n  72 |         The row-wise (squared) Euclidean norm of X.\n  73 |     \"\"\"\n  74 |     if sparse.issparse(X):\n  75 |         if not isinstance(X, sparse.csr_matrix):\n  76 |             X = sparse.csr_matrix(X)\n  77 |         norms = csr_row_norms(X)\n  78 |     else:\n  79 |         norms = np.einsum(\"ij,ij->i\", X, X)\n  80 | \n  81 |     if not squared:\n  82 |         np.sqrt(norms, norms)\n  83 |     return norms\n  84 | \n  85 | \n  86 | def fast_logdet(A):\n  87 |     \"\"\"Compute logarithm of determinant of a square matrix.\n  88 | \n  89 |     The (natural) logarithm of the determinant of a square matrix\n  90 |     is returned if det(A) is non-negative and well defined.\n  91 |     If the determinant is zero or negative returns -Inf.\n  92 | \n  93 |     Equivalent to : np.log(np.det(A)) but more robust.\n  94 | \n  95 |     Parameters\n  96 |     ----------\n  97 |     A : array_like of shape (n, n)\n  98 |         The square matrix.\n  99 | \n 100 |     Returns\n 101 |     -------\n 102 |     logdet : float\n 103 |         When det(A) is strictly positive, log(det(A)) is returned.\n 104 |         When det(A) is non-positive or not defined, then -inf is returned.\n 105 | \n 106 |     See Also\n 107 |     --------\n 108 |     numpy.linalg.slogdet : Compute the sign and (natural) logarithm of the determinant\n 109 |         of an array.\n 110 | \n 111 |     Examples\n 112 |     --------\n 113 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 114 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.extmath import fast_logdet\n 115 |     >>> a = np.array([[5, 1], [2, 8]])\n 116 |     >>> fast_logdet(a)\n 117 |     3.6375861597263857\n 118 |     \"\"\"\n 119 |     sign, ld = np.linalg.slogdet(A)\n 120 |     if not sign > 0:\n 121 |         return -np.inf\n 122 |     return ld\n 123 | \n 124 | \n 125 | def density(w, **kwargs):\n 126 |     \"\"\"Compute density of a sparse vector.\n 127 | \n 128 |     Parameters\n 129 |     ----------\n 130 |     w : array-like\n 131 |         The sparse vector.\n 132 |     **kwargs : keyword arguments\n 133 |         Ignored.\n 134 | \n 135 |         .. deprecated:: 1.2\n 136 |             ``**kwargs`` were deprecated in version 1.2 and will be removed in\n 137 |             1.4.\n 138 | \n 139 |     Returns\n 140 |     -------\n 141 |     float\n 142 |         The density of w, between 0 and 1.\n 143 |     \"\"\"\n 144 |     if kwargs:\n 145 |         warnings.warn(\n 146 |             (\n 147 |                 \"Additional keyword arguments are deprecated in version 1.2 and will be\"\n 148 |                 \" removed in version 1.4.\"\n 149 |             ),\n 150 |             FutureWarning,\n 151 |         )\n 152 | \n 153 |     if hasattr(w, \"toarray\"):\n 154 |         d = float(w.nnz) / (w.shape[0] * w.shape[1])\n 155 |     else:\n 156 |         d = 0 if w is None else float((w != 0).sum()) / w.size\n 157 |     return d\n 158 | \n 159 | \n 160 | def safe_sparse_dot(a, b, *, dense_output=False):\n 161 |     \"\"\"Dot product that handle the sparse matrix case correctly.\n 162 | \n 163 |     Parameters\n 164 |     ----------\n 165 |     a : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 166 |     b : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 167 |     dense_output : bool, default=False\n 168 |         When False, ``a`` and ``b`` both being sparse will yield sparse output.\n 169 |         When True, output will always be a dense array.\n 170 | \n 171 |     Returns\n 172 |     -------\n 173 |     dot_product : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 174 |         Sparse if ``a`` and ``b`` are sparse and ``dense_output=False``.\n 175 |     \"\"\"\n 176 |     if a.ndim > 2 or b.ndim > 2:\n 177 |         if sparse.issparse(a):\n 178 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies b is 3D+\n 179 |             # [i, j] @ [k, ..., l, m, n] -> [i, k, ..., l, n]\n 180 |             b_ = np.rollaxis(b, -2)\n 181 |             b_2d = b_.reshape((b.shape[-2], -1))\n 182 |             ret = a @ b_2d\n 183 |             ret = ret.reshape(a.shape[0], *b_.shape[1:])\n 184 |         elif sparse.issparse(b):\n 185 |             # sparse is always 2D. Implies a is 3D+\n 186 |             # [k, ..., l, m] @ [i, j] -> [k, ..., l, j]\n 187 |             a_2d = a.reshape(-1, a.shape[-1])\n 188 |             ret = a_2d @ b\n 189 |             ret = ret.reshape(*a.shape[:-1], b.shape[1])\n 190 |         else:\n 191 |             ret = np.dot(a, b)\n 192 |     else:\n 193 |         ret = a @ b\n 194 | \n 195 |     if (\n 196 |         sparse.issparse(a)\n 197 |         and sparse.issparse(b)\n 198 |         and dense_output\n 199 |         and hasattr(ret, \"toarray\")\n 200 |     ):\n 201 |         return ret.toarray()\n 202 |     return ret\n 203 | \n 204 | \n 205 | def randomized_range_finder(\n 206 |     A, *, size, n_iter, power_iteration_normalizer=\"auto\", random_state=None\n 207 | ):\n 208 |     \"\"\"Compute an orthonormal matrix whose range approximates the range of A.\n 209 | \n 210 |     Parameters\n 211 |     ----------\n 212 |     A : 2D array\n 213 |         The input data matrix.\n 214 | \n 215 |     size : int\n 216 |         Size of the return array.\n 217 | \n 218 |     n_iter : int\n 219 |         Number of power iterations used to stabilize the result.\n 220 | \n 221 |     power_iteration_normalizer : {'auto', 'QR', 'LU', 'none'}, default='auto'\n 222 |         Whether the power iterations are normalized with step-by-step\n 223 |         QR factorization (the slowest but most accurate), 'none'\n 224 |         (the fastest but numerically unstable when `n_iter` is large, e.g.\n 225 |         typically 5 or larger), or 'LU' factorization (numerically stable\n 226 |         but can lose slightly in accuracy). The 'auto' mode applies no\n 227 |         normalization if `n_iter` <= 2 and switches to LU otherwise.\n 228 | \n 229 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 230 | \n 231 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 232 |         The seed of the pseudo random number generator to use when shuffling\n 233 |         the data, i.e. getting the random vectors to initialize the algorithm.\n 234 |         Pass an int for reproducible results across multiple function calls.\n 235 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 236 | \n 237 |     Returns\n 238 |     -------\n 239 |     Q : ndarray\n 240 |         A (size x size) projection matrix, the range of which\n 241 |         approximates well the range of the input matrix A.\n 242 | \n 243 |     Notes\n 244 |     -----\n 245 | \n 246 |     Follows Algorithm 4.3 of\n 247 |     :arxiv:`\"Finding structure with randomness:\n 248 |     Stochastic algorithms for constructing approximate matrix decompositions\"\n 249 |     <0909.4061>`\n 250 |     Halko, et al. (2009)\n 251 | \n 252 |     An implementation of a randomized algorithm for principal component\n 253 |     analysis\n 254 |     A. Szlam et al. 2014\n 255 |     \"\"\"\n 256 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n 257 | \n 258 |     # Generating normal random vectors with shape: (A.shape[1], size)\n 259 |     Q = random_state.normal(size=(A.shape[1], size))\n 260 |     if hasattr(A, \"dtype\") and A.dtype.kind == \"f\":\n 261 |         # Ensure f32 is preserved as f32\n 262 |         Q = Q.astype(A.dtype, copy=False)\n 263 | \n 264 |     # Deal with \"auto\" mode\n 265 |     if power_iteration_normalizer == \"auto\":\n 266 |         if n_iter <= 2:\n 267 |             power_iteration_normalizer = \"none\"\n 268 |         else:\n 269 |             power_iteration_normalizer = \"LU\"\n 270 | \n 271 |     # Perform power iterations with Q to further 'imprint' the top\n 272 |     # singular vectors of A in Q\n 273 |     for i in range(n_iter):\n 274 |         if power_iteration_normalizer == \"none\":\n 275 |             Q = safe_sparse_dot(A, Q)\n 276 |             Q = safe_sparse_dot(A.T, Q)\n 277 |         elif power_iteration_normalizer == \"LU\":\n 278 |             Q, _ = linalg.lu(safe_sparse_dot(A, Q), permute_l=True)\n 279 |             Q, _ = linalg.lu(safe_sparse_dot(A.T, Q), permute_l=True)\n 280 |         elif power_iteration_normalizer == \"QR\":\n 281 |             Q, _ = linalg.qr(safe_sparse_dot(A, Q), mode=\"economic\")\n 282 |             Q, _ = linalg.qr(safe_sparse_dot(A.T, Q), mode=\"economic\")\n 283 | \n 284 |     # Sample the range of A using by linear projection of Q\n 285 |     # Extract an orthonormal basis\n 286 |     Q, _ = linalg.qr(safe_sparse_dot(A, Q), mode=\"economic\")\n 287 | \n 288 |     return Q\n 289 | \n 290 | \n 291 | def randomized_svd(\n 292 |     M,\n 293 |     n_components,\n 294 |     *,\n 295 |     n_oversamples=10,\n 296 |     n_iter=\"auto\",\n 297 |     power_iteration_normalizer=\"auto\",\n 298 |     transpose=\"auto\",\n 299 |     flip_sign=True,\n 300 |     random_state=None,\n 301 |     svd_lapack_driver=\"gesdd\",\n 302 | ):\n 303 |     \"\"\"Compute a truncated randomized SVD.\n 304 | \n 305 |     This method solves the fixed-rank approximation problem described in [1]_\n 306 |     (problem (1.5), p5).\n 307 | \n 308 |     Parameters\n 309 |     ----------\n 310 |     M : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 311 |         Matrix to decompose.\n 312 | \n 313 |     n_components : int\n 314 |         Number of singular values and vectors to extract.\n 315 | \n 316 |     n_oversamples : int, default=10\n 317 |         Additional number of random vectors to sample the range of M so as\n 318 |         to ensure proper conditioning. The total number of random vectors\n 319 |         used to find the range of M is n_components + n_oversamples. Smaller\n 320 |         number can improve speed but can negatively impact the quality of\n 321 |         approximation of singular vectors and singular values. Users might wish\n 322 |         to increase this parameter up to `2*k - n_components` where k is the\n 323 |         effective rank, for large matrices, noisy problems, matrices with\n 324 |         slowly decaying spectrums, or to increase precision accuracy. See [1]_\n 325 |         (pages 5, 23 and 26).\n 326 | \n 327 |     n_iter : int or 'auto', default='auto'\n 328 |         Number of power iterations. It can be used to deal with very noisy\n 329 |         problems. When 'auto', it is set to 4, unless `n_components` is small\n 330 |         (< .1 * min(X.shape)) in which case `n_iter` is set to 7.\n 331 |         This improves precision with few components. Note that in general\n 332 |         users should rather increase `n_oversamples` before increasing `n_iter`\n 333 |         as the principle of the randomized method is to avoid usage of these\n 334 |         more costly power iterations steps. When `n_components` is equal\n 335 |         or greater to the effective matrix rank and the spectrum does not\n 336 |         present a slow decay, `n_iter=0` or `1` should even work fine in theory\n 337 |         (see [1]_ page 9).\n 338 | \n 339 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.18\n 340 | \n 341 |     power_iteration_normalizer : {'auto', 'QR', 'LU', 'none'}, default='auto'\n 342 |         Whether the power iterations are normalized with step-by-step\n 343 |         QR factorization (the slowest but most accurate), 'none'\n 344 |         (the fastest but numerically unstable when `n_iter` is large, e.g.\n 345 |         typically 5 or larger), or 'LU' factorization (numerically stable\n 346 |         but can lose slightly in accuracy). The 'auto' mode applies no\n 347 |         normalization if `n_iter` <= 2 and switches to LU otherwise.\n 348 | \n 349 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n 350 | \n 351 |     transpose : bool or 'auto', default='auto'\n 352 |         Whether the algorithm should be applied to M.T instead of M. The\n 353 |         result should approximately be the same. The 'auto' mode will\n 354 |         trigger the transposition if M.shape[1] > M.shape[0] since this\n 355 |         implementation of randomized SVD tend to be a little faster in that\n 356 |         case.\n 357 | \n 358 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.18\n 359 | \n 360 |     flip_sign : bool, default=True\n 361 |         The output of a singular value decomposition is only unique up to a\n 362 |         permutation of the signs of the singular vectors. If `flip_sign` is\n 363 |         set to `True`, the sign ambiguity is resolved by making the largest\n 364 |         loadings for each component in the left singular vectors positive.\n 365 | \n 366 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default='warn'\n 367 |         The seed of the pseudo random number generator to use when\n 368 |         shuffling the data, i.e. getting the random vectors to initialize\n 369 |         the algorithm. Pass an int for reproducible results across multiple\n 370 |         function calls. See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 371 | \n 372 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.2\n 373 |             The default value changed from 0 to None.\n 374 | \n 375 |     svd_lapack_driver : {\"gesdd\", \"gesvd\"}, default=\"gesdd\"\n 376 |         Whether to use the more efficient divide-and-conquer approach\n 377 |         (`\"gesdd\"`) or more general rectangular approach (`\"gesvd\"`) to compute\n 378 |         the SVD of the matrix B, which is the projection of M into a low\n 379 |         dimensional subspace, as described in [1]_.\n 380 | \n 381 |         .. versionadded:: 1.2\n 382 | \n 383 |     Returns\n 384 |     -------\n 385 |     u : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 386 |         Unitary matrix having left singular vectors with signs flipped as columns.\n 387 |     s : ndarray of shape (n_components,)\n 388 |         The singular values, sorted in non-increasing order.\n 389 |     vh : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n 390 |         Unitary matrix having right singular vectors with signs flipped as rows.\n 391 | \n 392 |     Notes\n 393 |     -----\n 394 |     This algorithm finds a (usually very good) approximate truncated\n 395 |     singular value decomposition using randomization to speed up the\n 396 |     computations. It is particularly fast on large matrices on which\n 397 |     you wish to extract only a small number of components. In order to\n 398 |     obtain further speed up, `n_iter` can be set <=2 (at the cost of\n 399 |     loss of precision). To increase the precision it is recommended to\n 400 |     increase `n_oversamples`, up to `2*k-n_components` where k is the\n 401 |     effective rank. Usually, `n_components` is chosen to be greater than k\n 402 |     so increasing `n_oversamples` up to `n_components` should be enough.\n 403 | \n 404 |     References\n 405 |     ----------\n 406 |     .. [1] :arxiv:`\"Finding structure with randomness:\n 407 |       Stochastic algorithms for constructing approximate matrix decompositions\"\n 408 |       <0909.4061>`\n 409 |       Halko, et al. (2009)\n 410 | \n 411 |     .. [2] A randomized algorithm for the decomposition of matrices\n 412 |       Per-Gunnar Martinsson, Vladimir Rokhlin and Mark Tygert\n 413 | \n 414 |     .. [3] An implementation of a randomized algorithm for principal component\n 415 |       analysis A. Szlam et al. 2014\n 416 | \n 417 |     Examples\n 418 |     --------\n 419 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 420 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.extmath import randomized_svd\n 421 |     >>> a = np.array([[1, 2, 3, 5],\n 422 |     ...               [3, 4, 5, 6],\n 423 |     ...               [7, 8, 9, 10]])\n 424 |     >>> U, s, Vh = randomized_svd(a, n_components=2, random_state=0)\n 425 |     >>> U.shape, s.shape, Vh.shape\n 426 |     ((3, 2), (2,), (2, 4))\n 427 |     \"\"\"\n 428 |     if isinstance(M, (sparse.lil_matrix, sparse.dok_matrix)):\n 429 |         warnings.warn(\n 430 |             \"Calculating SVD of a {} is expensive. \"\n 431 |             \"csr_matrix is more efficient.\".format(type(M).__name__),\n 432 |             sparse.SparseEfficiencyWarning,\n 433 |         )\n 434 | \n 435 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n 436 |     n_random = n_components + n_oversamples\n 437 |     n_samples, n_features = M.shape\n 438 | \n 439 |     if n_iter == \"auto\":\n 440 |         # Checks if the number of iterations is explicitly specified\n 441 |         # Adjust n_iter. 7 was found a good compromise for PCA. See #5299\n 442 |         n_iter = 7 if n_components < 0.1 * min(M.shape) else 4\n 443 | \n 444 |     if transpose == \"auto\":\n 445 |         transpose = n_samples < n_features\n 446 |     if transpose:\n 447 |         # this implementation is a bit faster with smaller shape[1]\n 448 |         M = M.T\n 449 | \n 450 |     Q = randomized_range_finder(\n 451 |         M,\n 452 |         size=n_random,\n 453 |         n_iter=n_iter,\n 454 |         power_iteration_normalizer=power_iteration_normalizer,\n 455 |         random_state=random_state,\n 456 |     )\n 457 | \n 458 |     # project M to the (k + p) dimensional space using the basis vectors\n 459 |     B = safe_sparse_dot(Q.T, M)\n 460 | \n 461 |     # compute the SVD on the thin matrix: (k + p) wide\n 462 |     Uhat, s, Vt = linalg.svd(B, full_matrices=False, lapack_driver=svd_lapack_driver)\n 463 | \n 464 |     del B\n 465 |     U = np.dot(Q, Uhat)\n 466 | \n 467 |     if flip_sign:\n 468 |         if not transpose:\n 469 |             U, Vt = svd_flip(U, Vt)\n 470 |         else:\n 471 |             # In case of transpose u_based_decision=false\n 472 |             # to actually flip based on u and not v.\n 473 |             U, Vt = svd_flip(U, Vt, u_based_decision=False)\n 474 | \n 475 |     if transpose:\n 476 |         # transpose back the results according to the input convention\n 477 |         return Vt[:n_components, :].T, s[:n_components], U[:, :n_components].T\n 478 |     else:\n 479 |         return U[:, :n_components], s[:n_components], Vt[:n_components, :]\n 480 | \n 481 | \n 482 | def _randomized_eigsh(\n 483 |     M,\n 484 |     n_components,\n 485 |     *,\n 486 |     n_oversamples=10,\n 487 |     n_iter=\"auto\",\n 488 |     power_iteration_normalizer=\"auto\",\n 489 |     selection=\"module\",\n 490 |     random_state=None,\n 491 | ):\n 492 |     \"\"\"Computes a truncated eigendecomposition using randomized methods\n 493 | \n 494 |     This method solves the fixed-rank approximation problem described in the\n 495 |     Halko et al paper.\n 496 | \n 497 |     The choice of which components to select can be tuned with the `selection`\n 498 |     parameter.\n 499 | \n 500 |     .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 501 | \n 502 |     Parameters\n 503 |     ----------\n 504 |     M : ndarray or sparse matrix\n 505 |         Matrix to decompose, it should be real symmetric square or complex\n 506 |         hermitian\n 507 | \n 508 |     n_components : int\n 509 |         Number of eigenvalues and vectors to extract.\n 510 | \n 511 |     n_oversamples : int, default=10\n 512 |         Additional number of random vectors to sample the range of M so as\n 513 |         to ensure proper conditioning. The total number of random vectors\n 514 |         used to find the range of M is n_components + n_oversamples. Smaller\n 515 |         number can improve speed but can negatively impact the quality of\n 516 |         approximation of eigenvectors and eigenvalues. Users might wish\n 517 |         to increase this parameter up to `2*k - n_components` where k is the\n 518 |         effective rank, for large matrices, noisy problems, matrices with\n 519 |         slowly decaying spectrums, or to increase precision accuracy. See Halko\n 520 |         et al (pages 5, 23 and 26).\n 521 | \n 522 |     n_iter : int or 'auto', default='auto'\n 523 |         Number of power iterations. It can be used to deal with very noisy\n 524 |         problems. When 'auto', it is set to 4, unless `n_components` is small\n 525 |         (< .1 * min(X.shape)) in which case `n_iter` is set to 7.\n 526 |         This improves precision with few components. Note that in general\n 527 |         users should rather increase `n_oversamples` before increasing `n_iter`\n 528 |         as the principle of the randomized method is to avoid usage of these\n 529 |         more costly power iterations steps. When `n_components` is equal\n 530 |         or greater to the effective matrix rank and the spectrum does not\n 531 |         present a slow decay, `n_iter=0` or `1` should even work fine in theory\n 532 |         (see Halko et al paper, page 9).\n 533 | \n 534 |     power_iteration_normalizer : {'auto', 'QR', 'LU', 'none'}, default='auto'\n 535 |         Whether the power iterations are normalized with step-by-step\n 536 |         QR factorization (the slowest but most accurate), 'none'\n 537 |         (the fastest but numerically unstable when `n_iter` is large, e.g.\n 538 |         typically 5 or larger), or 'LU' factorization (numerically stable\n 539 |         but can lose slightly in accuracy). The 'auto' mode applies no\n 540 |         normalization if `n_iter` <= 2 and switches to LU otherwise.\n 541 | \n 542 |     selection : {'value', 'module'}, default='module'\n 543 |         Strategy used to select the n components. When `selection` is `'value'`\n 544 |         (not yet implemented, will become the default when implemented), the\n 545 |         components corresponding to the n largest eigenvalues are returned.\n 546 |         When `selection` is `'module'`, the components corresponding to the n\n 547 |         eigenvalues with largest modules are returned.\n 548 | \n 549 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance, default=None\n 550 |         The seed of the pseudo random number generator to use when shuffling\n 551 |         the data, i.e. getting the random vectors to initialize the algorithm.\n 552 |         Pass an int for reproducible results across multiple function calls.\n 553 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 554 | \n 555 |     Notes\n 556 |     -----\n 557 |     This algorithm finds a (usually very good) approximate truncated\n 558 |     eigendecomposition using randomized methods to speed up the computations.\n 559 | \n 560 |     This method is particularly fast on large matrices on which\n 561 |     you wish to extract only a small number of components. In order to\n 562 |     obtain further speed up, `n_iter` can be set <=2 (at the cost of\n 563 |     loss of precision). To increase the precision it is recommended to\n 564 |     increase `n_oversamples`, up to `2*k-n_components` where k is the\n 565 |     effective rank. Usually, `n_components` is chosen to be greater than k\n 566 |     so increasing `n_oversamples` up to `n_components` should be enough.\n 567 | \n 568 |     Strategy 'value': not implemented yet.\n 569 |     Algorithms 5.3, 5.4 and 5.5 in the Halko et al paper should provide good\n 570 |     condidates for a future implementation.\n 571 | \n 572 |     Strategy 'module':\n 573 |     The principle is that for diagonalizable matrices, the singular values and\n 574 |     eigenvalues are related: if t is an eigenvalue of A, then :math:`|t|` is a\n 575 |     singular value of A. This method relies on a randomized SVD to find the n\n 576 |     singular components corresponding to the n singular values with largest\n 577 |     modules, and then uses the signs of the singular vectors to find the true\n 578 |     sign of t: if the sign of left and right singular vectors are different\n 579 |     then the corresponding eigenvalue is negative.\n 580 | \n 581 |     Returns\n 582 |     -------\n 583 |     eigvals : 1D array of shape (n_components,) containing the `n_components`\n 584 |         eigenvalues selected (see ``selection`` parameter).\n 585 |     eigvecs : 2D array of shape (M.shape[0], n_components) containing the\n 586 |         `n_components` eigenvectors corresponding to the `eigvals`, in the\n 587 |         corresponding order. Note that this follows the `scipy.linalg.eigh`\n 588 |         convention.\n 589 | \n 590 |     See Also\n 591 |     --------\n 592 |     :func:`randomized_svd`\n 593 | \n 594 |     References\n 595 |     ----------\n 596 |     * :arxiv:`\"Finding structure with randomness:\n 597 |       Stochastic algorithms for constructing approximate matrix decompositions\"\n 598 |       (Algorithm 4.3 for strategy 'module') <0909.4061>`\n 599 |       Halko, et al. (2009)\n 600 |     \"\"\"\n 601 |     if selection == \"value\":  # pragma: no cover\n 602 |         # to do : an algorithm can be found in the Halko et al reference\n 603 |         raise NotImplementedError()\n 604 | \n 605 |     elif selection == \"module\":\n 606 |         # Note: no need for deterministic U and Vt (flip_sign=True),\n 607 |         # as we only use the dot product UVt afterwards\n 608 |         U, S, Vt = randomized_svd(\n 609 |             M,\n 610 |             n_components=n_components,\n 611 |             n_oversamples=n_oversamples,\n 612 |             n_iter=n_iter,\n 613 |             power_iteration_normalizer=power_iteration_normalizer,\n 614 |             flip_sign=False,\n 615 |             random_state=random_state,\n 616 |         )\n 617 | \n 618 |         eigvecs = U[:, :n_components]\n 619 |         eigvals = S[:n_components]\n 620 | \n 621 |         # Conversion of Singular values into Eigenvalues:\n 622 |         # For any eigenvalue t, the corresponding singular value is |t|.\n 623 |         # So if there is a negative eigenvalue t, the corresponding singular\n 624 |         # value will be -t, and the left (U) and right (V) singular vectors\n 625 |         # will have opposite signs.\n 626 |         # Fastest way: see <https://stackoverflow.com/a/61974002/7262247>\n 627 |         diag_VtU = np.einsum(\"ji,ij->j\", Vt[:n_components, :], U[:, :n_components])\n 628 |         signs = np.sign(diag_VtU)\n 629 |         eigvals = eigvals * signs\n 630 | \n 631 |     else:  # pragma: no cover\n 632 |         raise ValueError(\"Invalid `selection`: %r\" % selection)\n 633 | \n 634 |     return eigvals, eigvecs\n 635 | \n 636 | \n 637 | def weighted_mode(a, w, *, axis=0):\n 638 |     \"\"\"Return an array of the weighted modal (most common) value in the passed array.\n 639 | \n 640 |     If there is more than one such value, only the first is returned.\n 641 |     The bin-count for the modal bins is also returned.\n 642 | \n 643 |     This is an extension of the algorithm in scipy.stats.mode.\n 644 | \n 645 |     Parameters\n 646 |     ----------\n 647 |     a : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n 648 |         Array of which values to find mode(s).\n 649 |     w : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n 650 |         Array of weights for each value.\n 651 |     axis : int, default=0\n 652 |         Axis along which to operate. Default is 0, i.e. the first axis.\n 653 | \n 654 |     Returns\n 655 |     -------\n 656 |     vals : ndarray\n 657 |         Array of modal values.\n 658 |     score : ndarray\n 659 |         Array of weighted counts for each mode.\n 660 | \n 661 |     See Also\n 662 |     --------\n 663 |     scipy.stats.mode: Calculates the Modal (most common) value of array elements\n 664 |         along specified axis.\n 665 | \n 666 |     Examples\n 667 |     --------\n 668 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.extmath import weighted_mode\n 669 |     >>> x = [4, 1, 4, 2, 4, 2]\n 670 |     >>> weights = [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\n 671 |     >>> weighted_mode(x, weights)\n 672 |     (array([4.]), array([3.]))\n 673 | \n 674 |     The value 4 appears three times: with uniform weights, the result is\n 675 |     simply the mode of the distribution.\n 676 | \n 677 |     >>> weights = [1, 3, 0.5, 1.5, 1, 2]  # deweight the 4's\n 678 |     >>> weighted_mode(x, weights)\n 679 |     (array([2.]), array([3.5]))\n 680 | \n 681 |     The value 2 has the highest score: it appears twice with weights of\n 682 |     1.5 and 2: the sum of these is 3.5.\n 683 |     \"\"\"\n 684 |     if axis is None:\n 685 |         a = np.ravel(a)\n 686 |         w = np.ravel(w)\n 687 |         axis = 0\n 688 |     else:\n 689 |         a = np.asarray(a)\n 690 |         w = np.asarray(w)\n 691 | \n 692 |     if a.shape != w.shape:\n 693 |         w = np.full(a.shape, w, dtype=w.dtype)\n 694 | \n 695 |     scores = np.unique(np.ravel(a))  # get ALL unique values\n 696 |     testshape = list(a.shape)\n 697 |     testshape[axis] = 1\n 698 |     oldmostfreq = np.zeros(testshape)\n 699 |     oldcounts = np.zeros(testshape)\n 700 |     for score in scores:\n 701 |         template = np.zeros(a.shape)\n 702 |         ind = a == score\n 703 |         template[ind] = w[ind]\n 704 |         counts = np.expand_dims(np.sum(template, axis), axis)\n 705 |         mostfrequent = np.where(counts > oldcounts, score, oldmostfreq)\n 706 |         oldcounts = np.maximum(counts, oldcounts)\n 707 |         oldmostfreq = mostfrequent\n 708 |     return mostfrequent, oldcounts\n 709 | \n 710 | \n 711 | def cartesian(arrays, out=None):\n 712 |     \"\"\"Generate a cartesian product of input arrays.\n 713 | \n 714 |     Parameters\n 715 |     ----------\n 716 |     arrays : list of array-like\n 717 |         1-D arrays to form the cartesian product of.\n 718 |     out : ndarray of shape (M, len(arrays)), default=None\n 719 |         Array to place the cartesian product in.\n 720 | \n 721 |     Returns\n 722 |     -------\n 723 |     out : ndarray of shape (M, len(arrays))\n 724 |         Array containing the cartesian products formed of input arrays.\n 725 |         If not provided, the `dtype` of the output array is set to the most\n 726 |         permissive `dtype` of the input arrays, according to NumPy type\n 727 |         promotion.\n 728 | \n 729 |         .. versionadded:: 1.2\n 730 |            Add support for arrays of different types.\n 731 | \n 732 |     Notes\n 733 |     -----\n 734 |     This function may not be used on more than 32 arrays\n 735 |     because the underlying numpy functions do not support it.\n 736 | \n 737 |     Examples\n 738 |     --------\n 739 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.extmath import cartesian\n 740 |     >>> cartesian(([1, 2, 3], [4, 5], [6, 7]))\n 741 |     array([[1, 4, 6],\n 742 |            [1, 4, 7],\n 743 |            [1, 5, 6],\n 744 |            [1, 5, 7],\n 745 |            [2, 4, 6],\n 746 |            [2, 4, 7],\n 747 |            [2, 5, 6],\n 748 |            [2, 5, 7],\n 749 |            [3, 4, 6],\n 750 |            [3, 4, 7],\n 751 |            [3, 5, 6],\n 752 |            [3, 5, 7]])\n 753 |     \"\"\"\n 754 |     arrays = [np.asarray(x) for x in arrays]\n 755 |     shape = (len(x) for x in arrays)\n 756 | \n 757 |     ix = np.indices(shape)\n 758 |     ix = ix.reshape(len(arrays), -1).T\n 759 | \n 760 |     if out is None:\n 761 |         dtype = np.result_type(*arrays)  # find the most permissive dtype\n 762 |         out = np.empty_like(ix, dtype=dtype)\n 763 | \n 764 |     for n, arr in enumerate(arrays):\n 765 |         out[:, n] = arrays[n][ix[:, n]]\n 766 | \n 767 |     return out\n 768 | \n 769 | \n 770 | def svd_flip(u, v, u_based_decision=True):\n 771 |     \"\"\"Sign correction to ensure deterministic output from SVD.\n 772 | \n 773 |     Adjusts the columns of u and the rows of v such that the loadings in the\n 774 |     columns in u that are largest in absolute value are always positive.\n 775 | \n 776 |     Parameters\n 777 |     ----------\n 778 |     u : ndarray\n 779 |         Parameters u and v are the output of `linalg.svd` or\n 780 |         :func:`~sklearn.utils.extmath.randomized_svd`, with matching inner\n 781 |         dimensions so one can compute `np.dot(u * s, v)`.\n 782 | \n 783 |     v : ndarray\n 784 |         Parameters u and v are the output of `linalg.svd` or\n 785 |         :func:`~sklearn.utils.extmath.randomized_svd`, with matching inner\n 786 |         dimensions so one can compute `np.dot(u * s, v)`.\n 787 |         The input v should really be called vt to be consistent with scipy's\n 788 |         output.\n 789 | \n 790 |     u_based_decision : bool, default=True\n 791 |         If True, use the columns of u as the basis for sign flipping.\n 792 |         Otherwise, use the rows of v. The choice of which variable to base the\n 793 |         decision on is generally algorithm dependent.\n 794 | \n 795 |     Returns\n 796 |     -------\n 797 |     u_adjusted : ndarray\n 798 |         Array u with adjusted columns and the same dimensions as u.\n 799 | \n 800 |     v_adjusted : ndarray\n 801 |         Array v with adjusted rows and the same dimensions as v.\n 802 |     \"\"\"\n 803 |     if u_based_decision:\n 804 |         # columns of u, rows of v\n 805 |         max_abs_cols = np.argmax(np.abs(u), axis=0)\n 806 |         signs = np.sign(u[max_abs_cols, range(u.shape[1])])\n 807 |         u *= signs\n 808 |         v *= signs[:, np.newaxis]\n 809 |     else:\n 810 |         # rows of v, columns of u\n 811 |         max_abs_rows = np.argmax(np.abs(v), axis=1)\n 812 |         signs = np.sign(v[range(v.shape[0]), max_abs_rows])\n 813 |         u *= signs\n 814 |         v *= signs[:, np.newaxis]\n 815 |     return u, v\n 816 | \n 817 | \n 818 | def log_logistic(X, out=None):\n 819 |     \"\"\"Compute the log of the logistic function, ``log(1 / (1 + e ** -x))``.\n 820 | \n 821 |     This implementation is numerically stable because it splits positive and\n 822 |     negative values::\n 823 | \n 824 |         -log(1 + exp(-x_i))     if x_i > 0\n 825 |         x_i - log(1 + exp(x_i)) if x_i <= 0\n 826 | \n 827 |     For the ordinary logistic function, use ``scipy.special.expit``.\n 828 | \n 829 |     Parameters\n 830 |     ----------\n 831 |     X : array-like of shape (M, N) or (M,)\n 832 |         Argument to the logistic function.\n 833 | \n 834 |     out : array-like of shape (M, N) or (M,), default=None\n 835 |         Preallocated output array.\n 836 | \n 837 |     Returns\n 838 |     -------\n 839 |     out : ndarray of shape (M, N) or (M,)\n 840 |         Log of the logistic function evaluated at every point in x.\n 841 | \n 842 |     Notes\n 843 |     -----\n 844 |     See the blog post describing this implementation:\n 845 |     http://fa.bianp.net/blog/2013/numerical-optimizers-for-logistic-regression/\n 846 |     \"\"\"\n 847 |     is_1d = X.ndim == 1\n 848 |     X = np.atleast_2d(X)\n 849 |     X = check_array(X, dtype=np.float64)\n 850 | \n 851 |     n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n 852 | \n 853 |     if out is None:\n 854 |         out = np.empty_like(X)\n 855 | \n 856 |     _log_logistic_sigmoid(n_samples, n_features, X, out)\n 857 | \n 858 |     if is_1d:\n 859 |         return np.squeeze(out)\n 860 |     return out\n 861 | \n 862 | \n 863 | def softmax(X, copy=True):\n 864 |     \"\"\"\n 865 |     Calculate the softmax function.\n 866 | \n 867 |     The softmax function is calculated by\n 868 |     np.exp(X) / np.sum(np.exp(X), axis=1)\n 869 | \n 870 |     This will cause overflow when large values are exponentiated.\n 871 |     Hence the largest value in each row is subtracted from each data\n 872 |     point to prevent this.\n 873 | \n 874 |     Parameters\n 875 |     ----------\n 876 |     X : array-like of float of shape (M, N)\n 877 |         Argument to the logistic function.\n 878 | \n 879 |     copy : bool, default=True\n 880 |         Copy X or not.\n 881 | \n 882 |     Returns\n 883 |     -------\n 884 |     out : ndarray of shape (M, N)\n 885 |         Softmax function evaluated at every point in x.\n 886 |     \"\"\"\n 887 |     xp, is_array_api_compliant = get_namespace(X)\n 888 |     if copy:\n 889 |         X = xp.asarray(X, copy=True)\n 890 |     max_prob = xp.reshape(xp.max(X, axis=1), (-1, 1))\n 891 |     X -= max_prob\n 892 | \n 893 |     if _is_numpy_namespace(xp):\n 894 |         # optimization for NumPy arrays\n 895 |         np.exp(X, out=np.asarray(X))\n 896 |     else:\n 897 |         # array_api does not have `out=`\n 898 |         X = xp.exp(X)\n 899 | \n 900 |     sum_prob = xp.reshape(xp.sum(X, axis=1), (-1, 1))\n 901 |     X /= sum_prob\n 902 |     return X\n 903 | \n 904 | \n 905 | def make_nonnegative(X, min_value=0):\n 906 |     \"\"\"Ensure `X.min()` >= `min_value`.\n 907 | \n 908 |     Parameters\n 909 |     ----------\n 910 |     X : array-like\n 911 |         The matrix to make non-negative.\n 912 |     min_value : float, default=0\n 913 |         The threshold value.\n 914 | \n 915 |     Returns\n 916 |     -------\n 917 |     array-like\n 918 |         The thresholded array.\n 919 | \n 920 |     Raises\n 921 |     ------\n 922 |     ValueError\n 923 |         When X is sparse.\n 924 |     \"\"\"\n 925 |     min_ = X.min()\n 926 |     if min_ < min_value:\n 927 |         if sparse.issparse(X):\n 928 |             raise ValueError(\n 929 |                 \"Cannot make the data matrix\"\n 930 |                 \" nonnegative because it is sparse.\"\n 931 |                 \" Adding a value to every entry would\"\n 932 |                 \" make it no longer sparse.\"\n 933 |             )\n 934 |         X = X + (min_value - min_)\n 935 |     return X\n 936 | \n 937 | \n 938 | # Use at least float64 for the accumulating functions to avoid precision issue\n 939 | # see https://github.com/numpy/numpy/issues/9393. The float64 is also retained\n 940 | # as it is in case the float overflows\n 941 | def _safe_accumulator_op(op, x, *args, **kwargs):\n 942 |     \"\"\"\n 943 |     This function provides numpy accumulator functions with a float64 dtype\n 944 |     when used on a floating point input. This prevents accumulator overflow on\n 945 |     smaller floating point dtypes.\n 946 | \n 947 |     Parameters\n 948 |     ----------\n 949 |     op : function\n 950 |         A numpy accumulator function such as np.mean or np.sum.\n 951 |     x : ndarray\n 952 |         A numpy array to apply the accumulator function.\n 953 |     *args : positional arguments\n 954 |         Positional arguments passed to the accumulator function after the\n 955 |         input x.\n 956 |     **kwargs : keyword arguments\n 957 |         Keyword arguments passed to the accumulator function.\n 958 | \n 959 |     Returns\n 960 |     -------\n 961 |     result\n 962 |         The output of the accumulator function passed to this function.\n 963 |     \"\"\"\n 964 |     if np.issubdtype(x.dtype, np.floating) and x.dtype.itemsize < 8:\n 965 |         result = op(x, *args, **kwargs, dtype=np.float64)\n 966 |     else:\n 967 |         result = op(x, *args, **kwargs)\n 968 |     return result\n 969 | \n 970 | \n 971 | def _incremental_mean_and_var(\n 972 |     X, last_mean, last_variance, last_sample_count, sample_weight=None\n 973 | ):\n 974 |     \"\"\"Calculate mean update and a Youngs and Cramer variance update.\n 975 | \n 976 |     If sample_weight is given, the weighted mean and variance is computed.\n 977 | \n 978 |     Update a given mean and (possibly) variance according to new data given\n 979 |     in X. last_mean is always required to compute the new mean.\n 980 |     If last_variance is None, no variance is computed and None return for\n 981 |     updated_variance.\n 982 | \n 983 |     From the paper \"Algorithms for computing the sample variance: analysis and\n 984 |     recommendations\", by Chan, Golub, and LeVeque.\n 985 | \n 986 |     Parameters\n 987 |     ----------\n 988 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 989 |         Data to use for variance update.\n 990 | \n 991 |     last_mean : array-like of shape (n_features,)\n 992 | \n 993 |     last_variance : array-like of shape (n_features,)\n 994 | \n 995 |     last_sample_count : array-like of shape (n_features,)\n 996 |         The number of samples encountered until now if sample_weight is None.\n 997 |         If sample_weight is not None, this is the sum of sample_weight\n 998 |         encountered.\n 999 | \n1000 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or None\n1001 |         Sample weights. If None, compute the unweighted mean/variance.\n1002 | \n1003 |     Returns\n1004 |     -------\n1005 |     updated_mean : ndarray of shape (n_features,)\n1006 | \n1007 |     updated_variance : ndarray of shape (n_features,)\n1008 |         None if last_variance was None.\n1009 | \n1010 |     updated_sample_count : ndarray of shape (n_features,)\n1011 | \n1012 |     Notes\n1013 |     -----\n1014 |     NaNs are ignored during the algorithm.\n1015 | \n1016 |     References\n1017 |     ----------\n1018 |     T. Chan, G. Golub, R. LeVeque. Algorithms for computing the sample\n1019 |         variance: recommendations, The American Statistician, Vol. 37, No. 3,\n1020 |         pp. 242-247\n1021 | \n1022 |     Also, see the sparse implementation of this in\n1023 |     `utils.sparsefuncs.incr_mean_variance_axis` and\n1024 |     `utils.sparsefuncs_fast.incr_mean_variance_axis0`\n1025 |     \"\"\"\n1026 |     # old = stats until now\n1027 |     # new = the current increment\n1028 |     # updated = the aggregated stats\n1029 |     last_sum = last_mean * last_sample_count\n1030 |     X_nan_mask = np.isnan(X)\n1031 |     if np.any(X_nan_mask):\n1032 |         sum_op = np.nansum\n1033 |     else:\n1034 |         sum_op = np.sum\n1035 |     if sample_weight is not None:\n1036 |         # equivalent to np.nansum(X * sample_weight, axis=0)\n1037 |         # safer because np.float64(X*W) != np.float64(X)*np.float64(W)\n1038 |         new_sum = _safe_accumulator_op(\n1039 |             np.matmul, sample_weight, np.where(X_nan_mask, 0, X)\n1040 |         )\n1041 |         new_sample_count = _safe_accumulator_op(\n1042 |             np.sum, sample_weight[:, None] * (~X_nan_mask), axis=0\n1043 |         )\n1044 |     else:\n1045 |         new_sum = _safe_accumulator_op(sum_op, X, axis=0)\n1046 |         n_samples = X.shape[0]\n1047 |         new_sample_count = n_samples - np.sum(X_nan_mask, axis=0)\n1048 | \n1049 |     updated_sample_count = last_sample_count + new_sample_count\n1050 | \n1051 |     updated_mean = (last_sum + new_sum) / updated_sample_count\n1052 | \n1053 |     if last_variance is None:\n1054 |         updated_variance = None\n1055 |     else:\n1056 |         T = new_sum / new_sample_count\n1057 |         temp = X - T\n1058 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n1059 |             # equivalent to np.nansum((X-T)**2 * sample_weight, axis=0)\n1060 |             # safer because np.float64(X*W) != np.float64(X)*np.float64(W)\n1061 |             correction = _safe_accumulator_op(\n1062 |                 np.matmul, sample_weight, np.where(X_nan_mask, 0, temp)\n1063 |             )\n1064 |             temp **= 2\n1065 |             new_unnormalized_variance = _safe_accumulator_op(\n1066 |                 np.matmul, sample_weight, np.where(X_nan_mask, 0, temp)\n1067 |             )\n1068 |         else:\n1069 |             correction = _safe_accumulator_op(sum_op, temp, axis=0)\n1070 |             temp **= 2\n1071 |             new_unnormalized_variance = _safe_accumulator_op(sum_op, temp, axis=0)\n1072 | \n1073 |         # correction term of the corrected 2 pass algorithm.\n1074 |         # See \"Algorithms for computing the sample variance: analysis\n1075 |         # and recommendations\", by Chan, Golub, and LeVeque.\n1076 |         new_unnormalized_variance -= correction**2 / new_sample_count\n1077 | \n1078 |         last_unnormalized_variance = last_variance * last_sample_count\n1079 | \n1080 |         with np.errstate(divide=\"ignore\", invalid=\"ignore\"):\n1081 |             last_over_new_count = last_sample_count / new_sample_count\n1082 |             updated_unnormalized_variance = (\n1083 |                 last_unnormalized_variance\n1084 |                 + new_unnormalized_variance\n1085 |                 + last_over_new_count\n1086 |                 / updated_sample_count\n1087 |                 * (last_sum / last_over_new_count - new_sum) ** 2\n1088 |             )\n1089 | \n1090 |         zeros = last_sample_count == 0\n1091 |         updated_unnormalized_variance[zeros] = new_unnormalized_variance[zeros]\n1092 |         updated_variance = updated_unnormalized_variance / updated_sample_count\n1093 | \n1094 |     return updated_mean, updated_variance, updated_sample_count\n1095 | \n1096 | \n1097 | def _deterministic_vector_sign_flip(u):\n1098 |     \"\"\"Modify the sign of vectors for reproducibility.\n1099 | \n1100 |     Flips the sign of elements of all the vectors (rows of u) such that\n1101 |     the absolute maximum element of each vector is positive.\n1102 | \n1103 |     Parameters\n1104 |     ----------\n1105 |     u : ndarray\n1106 |         Array with vectors as its rows.\n1107 | \n1108 |     Returns\n1109 |     -------\n1110 |     u_flipped : ndarray with same shape as u\n1111 |         Array with the sign flipped vectors as its rows.\n1112 |     \"\"\"\n1113 |     max_abs_rows = np.argmax(np.abs(u), axis=1)\n1114 |     signs = np.sign(u[range(u.shape[0]), max_abs_rows])\n1115 |     u *= signs[:, np.newaxis]\n1116 |     return u\n1117 | \n1118 | \n1119 | def stable_cumsum(arr, axis=None, rtol=1e-05, atol=1e-08):\n1120 |     \"\"\"Use high precision for cumsum and check that final value matches sum.\n1121 | \n1122 |     Warns if the final cumulative sum does not match the sum (up to the chosen\n1123 |     tolerance).\n1124 | \n1125 |     Parameters\n1126 |     ----------\n1127 |     arr : array-like\n1128 |         To be cumulatively summed as flat.\n1129 |     axis : int, default=None\n1130 |         Axis along which the cumulative sum is computed.\n1131 |         The default (None) is to compute the cumsum over the flattened array.\n1132 |     rtol : float, default=1e-05\n1133 |         Relative tolerance, see ``np.allclose``.\n1134 |     atol : float, default=1e-08\n1135 |         Absolute tolerance, see ``np.allclose``.\n1136 | \n1137 |     Returns\n1138 |     -------\n1139 |     out : ndarray\n1140 |         Array with the cumulative sums along the chosen axis.\n1141 |     \"\"\"\n1142 |     out = np.cumsum(arr, axis=axis, dtype=np.float64)\n1143 |     expected = np.sum(arr, axis=axis, dtype=np.float64)\n1144 |     if not np.all(\n1145 |         np.isclose(\n1146 |             out.take(-1, axis=axis), expected, rtol=rtol, atol=atol, equal_nan=True\n1147 |         )\n1148 |     ):\n1149 |         warnings.warn(\n1150 |             (\n1151 |                 \"cumsum was found to be unstable: \"\n1152 |                 \"its last element does not correspond to sum\"\n1153 |             ),\n1154 |             RuntimeWarning,\n1155 |         )\n1156 |     return out\n1157 | \n1158 | \n1159 | def _nanaverage(a, weights=None):\n1160 |     \"\"\"Compute the weighted average, ignoring NaNs.\n1161 | \n1162 |     Parameters\n1163 |     ----------\n1164 |     a : ndarray\n1165 |         Array containing data to be averaged.\n1166 |     weights : array-like, default=None\n1167 |         An array of weights associated with the values in a. Each value in a\n1168 |         contributes to the average according to its associated weight. The\n1169 |         weights array can either be 1-D of the same shape as a. If `weights=None`,\n1170 |         then all data in a are assumed to have a weight equal to one.\n1171 | \n1172 |     Returns\n1173 |     -------\n1174 |     weighted_average : float\n1175 |         The weighted average.\n1176 | \n1177 |     Notes\n1178 |     -----\n1179 |     This wrapper to combine :func:`numpy.average` and :func:`numpy.nanmean`, so\n1180 |     that :func:`np.nan` values are ignored from the average and weights can\n1181 |     be passed. Note that when possible, we delegate to the prime methods.\n1182 |     \"\"\"\n1183 | \n1184 |     if len(a) == 0:\n1185 |         return np.nan\n1186 | \n1187 |     mask = np.isnan(a)\n1188 |     if mask.all():\n1189 |         return np.nan\n1190 | \n1191 |     if weights is None:\n1192 |         return np.nanmean(a)\n1193 | \n1194 |     weights = np.array(weights, copy=False)\n1195 |     a, weights = a[~mask], weights[~mask]\n1196 |     try:\n1197 |         return np.average(a, weights=weights)\n1198 |     except ZeroDivisionError:\n1199 |         # this is when all weights are zero, then ignore them\n1200 |         return np.average(a)\n1201 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "After thorough analysis of the codebase and verification of the dependencies, I can confirm that the previously identified locations are complete and sufficient to implement the sparse matrix output feature for SplineTransformer. Let me refine the locations block to ensure it's in the required format:\n\n```\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 704\nline: 719-721\nline: 862\nline: 910\nline: 914\nline: 921\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: __init__\nfunction: fit\nfunction: transform\nfunction: _more_tags\n\nsklearn/utils/extmath.py\nline: 160\nline: 161-202\nfunction: safe_sparse_dot\n\nsklearn/utils/_set_output.py\nline: 139\nfunction: _wrap_data_to_container\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 579\nclass: TransformerMixin\nfunction: fit_transform\n\nscipy/interpolate/_bsplines.py\nline: 10\nline: 23\nclass: BSpline\nfunction: construct_fast\n```\n\nThis location block provides all necessary information to implement the sparse matrix output feature for SplineTransformer. The files and functions listed cover:\n1. The main SplineTransformer class where modifications are needed\n2. The sparse matrix handling utilities available in scikit-learn\n3. The transformer mixin that provides base functionality\n4. The BSpline implementation that will be used with the sparse feature\n\nThe implementation would follow this flow:\n1. Add sparse parameter to SplineTransformer.__init__\n2. Update validation constraints in _parameter_constraints\n3. Modify the coefficient matrix creation in fit method to handle sparse cases\n4. Update transform method to return appropriate sparse matrix output\n5. Utilize safe_sparse_dot from extmath.py for efficient sparse computations\n6. Ensure proper integration with scikit-learn's transformer infrastructure\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_data_dependencies", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n- '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.  \n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nArgs:\n- `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names. \n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes. \n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`. \n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope. \n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. \n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n- `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols. \n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n\nImportant behavior:\n- If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n- If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n- `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n- `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n- `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n- `symbol`: The symbol name.\n- `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n- `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n\nEach edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"symbol_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.", "default": null}, "scope_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.", "default": null}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.", "default": null}, "edge_type": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 12, "description": "Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice."}, "path_limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 4, "description": "Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 457045, "completion_tokens": 1264}, "time": 410.9048819541931}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30327", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py", "sklearn/base.py", "sklearn/utils/__init__.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_estimator_tags_renamed", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_0_22_0.py:test_sklearn_compatible_estimator", "sklearn/gaussian_process/kernels.py:Kernel", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator", "examples/ensemble/plot_adaboost_multiclass.py:misclassification_error", "sklearn/utils/parallel.py:delayed", "examples/compose/plot_column_transformer.py:text_stats", "examples/applications/plot_out_of_core_classification.py:ReutersParser", "sklearn/utils/__init__.py:__getattr__"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_estimator_tags_renamed", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.__str__", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD.__sklearn_tags__", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster.update", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version.__repr__", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture._get_parameters", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector._get_support_mask", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException.__str__", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning.__str__", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark.make_scorers", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark.skip", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit.symmetrize_raw_prediction", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit.link", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_0_22_0.py:test_sklearn_compatible_estimator", "sklearn/gaussian_process/kernels.py:Kernel.n_dims", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__", "examples/ensemble/plot_adaboost_multiclass.py:misclassification_error", "sklearn/utils/parallel.py:delayed.delayed_function", "examples/compose/plot_column_transformer.py:text_stats", "examples/applications/plot_out_of_core_classification.py:ReutersParser.end_title", "sklearn/utils/__init__.py:__getattr__"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 247\nline: 293-334\nfunction: get_tags\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 247-290\nfunction: default_tags\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 4447\nline: 4453-4466\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 560\nfunction: BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 106-112\nfunction: __getattr__\n```\n\nThese locations are ordered by importance, with the most critical files and functions listed first. The key modifications needed are:\n\n1. `sklearn/utils/_tags.py`: This is the main file that needs changes to implement the tag conversion functionality. Specifically:\n   - The `get_tags` function needs to be updated to handle both old and new tags\n   - The `default_tags` function needs modifications to support tag conversion\n   - The `TargetTags`, `ClassifierTags`, and `RegressorTags` classes may need updates to support the new tag system\n\n2. `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py`: This file contains the `check_estimator_tags_renamed` function that needs to be modified to warn about deprecated usage of `_get_tags` and `_more_tags`.\n\n3. `sklearn/base.py`: The `BaseEstimator` class may need modifications to properly handle tag inheritance and detection.\n\n4. `sklearn/utils/__init__.py`: May need updates to expose the new tagging system while maintaining backward compatibility.\n\nThe core changes will involve modifying the tag handling logic to properly detect and convert between old and new tag systems, while maintaining proper warnings and deprecation handling as requested in the issue description."], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "90a51c12d6159f72ab8990ea1a3ff835861ec81d", "problem_statement": "MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\r\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/base.py b/sklearn/base.py\nindex d646f8d3e56bf..2c82cf05a6c5a 100644\n--- a/sklearn/base.py\n+++ b/sklearn/base.py\n@@ -389,6 +389,33 @@ def __setstate__(self, state):\n         except AttributeError:\n             self.__dict__.update(state)\n \n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _more_tags(self):\n+        \"\"\"This code should never be reached since our `get_tags` will fallback on\n+        `__sklearn_tags__` implemented below. We keep it for backward compatibility.\n+        It is tested in `test_base_estimator_more_tags` in\n+        `sklearn/utils/testing/test_tags.py`.\"\"\"\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, default_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_more_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+        return _to_old_tags(default_tags(self))\n+\n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _get_tags(self):\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, get_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_get_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+\n+        return _to_old_tags(get_tags(self))\n+\n     def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n         return Tags(\n             estimator_type=None,\ndiff --git a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\nindex ccbc9d2438268..1ba1913c37234 100644\n--- a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n+++ b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import warnings\n+from collections import OrderedDict\n from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from itertools import chain\n \n from .fixes import _dataclass_args\n \n@@ -290,6 +292,71 @@ def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     )\n \n \n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _find_tags_provider(estimator, warn=True):\n+    \"\"\"Find the tags provider for an estimator.\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    estimator : estimator object\n+        The estimator to find the tags provider for.\n+\n+    warn : bool, default=True\n+        Whether to warn if the tags provider is not found.\n+\n+    Returns\n+    -------\n+    tag_provider : str\n+        The tags provider for the estimator. Can be one of:\n+        - \"_get_tags\": to use the old tags infrastructure\n+        - \"__sklearn_tags__\": to use the new tags infrastructure\n+    \"\"\"\n+    mro_model = type(estimator).mro()\n+    tags_mro = OrderedDict()\n+    for klass in mro_model:\n+        tags_provider = []\n+        if \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_more_tags\")\n+        if \"_get_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_get_tags\")\n+        if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"__sklearn_tags__\")\n+        tags_mro[klass.__name__] = tags_provider\n+\n+    all_providers = set(chain.from_iterable(tags_mro.values()))\n+    if \"__sklearn_tags__\" not in all_providers:\n+        # default on the old tags infrastructure\n+        return \"_get_tags\"\n+\n+    tag_provider = \"__sklearn_tags__\"\n+    for klass in tags_mro:\n+        has_get_or_more_tags = any(\n+            provider in tags_mro[klass] for provider in (\"_get_tags\", \"_more_tags\")\n+        )\n+        has_sklearn_tags = \"__sklearn_tags__\" in tags_mro[klass]\n+\n+        if tags_mro[klass] and tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":  # is it empty\n+            if has_get_or_more_tags and not has_sklearn_tags:\n+                # Case where a class does not implement __sklearn_tags__ and we fallback\n+                # to _get_tags. We should therefore warn for implementing\n+                # __sklearn_tags__.\n+                tag_provider = \"_get_tags\"\n+                break\n+\n+    if warn and tag_provider == \"_get_tags\":\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            f\"The {estimator.__class__.__name__} or classes from which it inherits \"\n+            \"use `_get_tags` and `_more_tags`. Please define the \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` method, or inherit from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` \"\n+            \"and/or other appropriate mixins such as `sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, `sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`. From scikit-learn 1.7, not defining \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` will raise an error.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+    return tag_provider\n+\n+\n def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n \n@@ -316,19 +383,201 @@ def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n         The estimator tags.\n     \"\"\"\n \n-    if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n+    tag_provider = _find_tags_provider(estimator)\n+\n+    if tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":\n         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n     else:\n-        warnings.warn(\n-            f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n-            \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n-            \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n-            \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n-            category=FutureWarning,\n+        # TODO(1.7): Remove this branch of the code\n+        # Let's go through the MRO and patch each class implementing _more_tags\n+        sklearn_tags_provider = {}\n+        more_tags_provider = {}\n+        class_order = []\n+        for klass in reversed(type(estimator).mro()):\n+            if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+                sklearn_tags_provider[klass] = klass.__sklearn_tags__(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+            elif \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+                more_tags_provider[klass] = klass._more_tags(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+\n+        # Find differences between consecutive in the case of __sklearn_tags__\n+        # inheritance\n+        sklearn_tags_diff = {}\n+        items = list(sklearn_tags_provider.items())\n+        for current_item, next_item in zip(items[:-1], items[1:]):\n+            current_name, current_tags = current_item\n+            next_name, next_tags = next_item\n+            current_tags = _to_old_tags(current_tags)\n+            next_tags = _to_old_tags(next_tags)\n+\n+            # Compare tags and store differences\n+            diff = {}\n+            for key in current_tags:\n+                if current_tags[key] != next_tags[key]:\n+                    diff[key] = next_tags[key]\n+\n+            sklearn_tags_diff[next_name] = diff\n+\n+        tags = {}\n+        for klass in class_order:\n+            if klass in sklearn_tags_diff:\n+                tags.update(sklearn_tags_diff[klass])\n+            elif klass in more_tags_provider:\n+                tags.update(more_tags_provider[klass])\n+\n+        tags = _to_new_tags(\n+            {**_to_old_tags(default_tags(estimator)), **tags}, estimator\n         )\n-        tags = default_tags(estimator)\n \n     return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _safe_tags(estimator, key=None):\n+    warnings.warn(\n+        \"The `_safe_tags` function is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+        \"1.7. Use the public `get_tags` function instead and make sure to implement \"\n+        \"the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+        category=FutureWarning,\n+    )\n+    tags = _to_old_tags(get_tags(estimator))\n+\n+    if key is not None:\n+        if key not in tags:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                f\"The key {key} is not defined for the class \"\n+                f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__}.\"\n+            )\n+        return tags[key]\n+    return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_new_tags(old_tags, estimator=None):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    input_tags = InputTags(\n+        one_d_array=\"1darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_array=\"2darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        three_d_array=\"3darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        sparse=\"sparse\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        categorical=\"categorical\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        string=\"string\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        dict=\"dict\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_X\"],\n+        allow_nan=old_tags[\"allow_nan\"],\n+        pairwise=old_tags[\"pairwise\"],\n+    )\n+    target_tags = TargetTags(\n+        required=old_tags[\"requires_y\"],\n+        one_d_labels=\"1dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_labels=\"2dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_y\"],\n+        multi_output=old_tags[\"multioutput\"] or old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+        single_output=not old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+    )\n+    if estimator is not None and (\n+        hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n+    ):\n+        transformer_tags = TransformerTags(\n+            preserves_dtype=old_tags[\"preserves_dtype\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        transformer_tags = None\n+    estimator_type = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None)\n+    if estimator_type == \"classifier\":\n+        classifier_tags = ClassifierTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_class=not old_tags[\"binary_only\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        classifier_tags = None\n+    if estimator_type == \"regressor\":\n+        regressor_tags = RegressorTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        regressor_tags = None\n+    return Tags(\n+        estimator_type=estimator_type,\n+        target_tags=target_tags,\n+        transformer_tags=transformer_tags,\n+        classifier_tags=classifier_tags,\n+        regressor_tags=regressor_tags,\n+        input_tags=input_tags,\n+        array_api_support=old_tags[\"array_api_support\"],\n+        no_validation=old_tags[\"no_validation\"],\n+        non_deterministic=old_tags[\"non_deterministic\"],\n+        requires_fit=old_tags[\"requires_fit\"],\n+        _skip_test=old_tags[\"_skip_test\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_old_tags(new_tags):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    if new_tags.classifier_tags:\n+        binary_only = not new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_class\n+        multilabel_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        binary_only = False\n+        multilabel_clf = False\n+        poor_score_clf = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.regressor_tags:\n+        multilabel_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        multilabel_reg = False\n+        poor_score_reg = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.transformer_tags:\n+        preserves_dtype = new_tags.transformer_tags.preserves_dtype\n+    else:\n+        preserves_dtype = [\"float64\"]\n+\n+    tags = {\n+        \"allow_nan\": new_tags.input_tags.allow_nan,\n+        \"array_api_support\": new_tags.array_api_support,\n+        \"binary_only\": binary_only,\n+        \"multilabel\": multilabel_clf or multilabel_reg,\n+        \"multioutput\": new_tags.target_tags.multi_output,\n+        \"multioutput_only\": (\n+            not new_tags.target_tags.single_output and new_tags.target_tags.multi_output\n+        ),\n+        \"no_validation\": new_tags.no_validation,\n+        \"non_deterministic\": new_tags.non_deterministic,\n+        \"pairwise\": new_tags.input_tags.pairwise,\n+        \"preserves_dtype\": preserves_dtype,\n+        \"poor_score\": poor_score_clf or poor_score_reg,\n+        \"requires_fit\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+        \"requires_positive_X\": new_tags.input_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"requires_y\": new_tags.target_tags.required,\n+        \"requires_positive_y\": new_tags.target_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"_skip_test\": new_tags._skip_test,\n+        \"stateless\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+    }\n+    X_types = []\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.one_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"1darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.two_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"2darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.three_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"3darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.sparse:\n+        X_types.append(\"sparse\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.categorical:\n+        X_types.append(\"categorical\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.string:\n+        X_types.append(\"string\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.dict:\n+        X_types.append(\"dict\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.one_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"1dlabels\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.two_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"2dlabels\")\n+    tags[\"X_types\"] = X_types\n+    return tags\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 219986, "completion_tokens": 677}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #5: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #5 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #6: get_trace_data_dependencies ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n- '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.  \n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nArgs:\n- `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names. \n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes. \n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`. \n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope. \n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. \n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n- `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols. \n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n\nImportant behavior:\n- If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n- If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n- `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n- `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n- `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n- `symbol`: The symbol name.\n- `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n- `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n\nEach edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) symbol_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n  (2) scope_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n  (3) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n  (4) edge_type (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.\n  (5) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges.\n  (6) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice.\n  (7) path_limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include.\n---- END FUNCTION #6 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - If execution-trace or data-dependency tools are available, use them as compact hints to complement your understanding of the execution flow.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\n\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental evidence from software dynamic analysis is available through a tool for this instance: `get_trace_artifacts` and `get_trace_data_dependencies`. \nThese tools provide compact raw slices of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts and data-dependency evidence, respectively:\n- `get_trace_artifacts`: The unique execution trace artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis. \n    This analysis involves executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled, \n    tracing their execution, and subtracting the overlapping parts from execution trace of A.py. \n    The tool returns the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n- `get_trace_data_dependencies`: The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts. \n    It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nSuggested workflow:\n- At the beginning of analyzing a new data point, first inspect the raw execution-trace evidence with `get_trace_artifacts`.\n- During the analysis, you may call `get_trace_artifacts` again with narrower filters such as `path_contains` or `name_contains`.\n- When available, use `get_trace_data_dependencies` whenever you need a more precise dataflow slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- You can revisit these tools at any point in the investigation; they are not only for the beginning.\n- Treat these tool outputs as compact raw evidence and verify them with the regular repository tools before finishing.\n\nNote: \n- Use the supplemental evidence to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself."}, {"content": "I'll help analyze this feature request systematically using the available tools.\n\nLet me start by exploring the repository structure to locate relevant files:\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['/']</parameter>\n<parameter=traversal_depth>2</parameter>\n<parameter=dependency_type_filter>['contains']</parameter>\n<parameter=entity_type_filter>['file', 'directory']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id"